course: dictionary
lesson: 12393
title: Cumulatively: adding it all up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cumulatively is a long word with a simple job. It tells you to add things up, one after another, so that each new amount goes on top of the ones before it. «Cumulatively, the team scored 200 goals» means all the goals from every game, added together.
explain: Think of a piggy bank. You put in 2 pounds on Monday and 3 pounds on Tuesday. Cumulatively, you have saved 5 pounds. Each day's money is added to the total from before.
explain: Look for clues. Words like «all together», «in total» or «over time» show that cumulatively is near. «Small savings, cumulatively, make a big difference.» Ask yourself: are the amounts building up into a bigger total? If yes, cumulatively is the right meaning.
fact: Cumulatively means taken all together, with each new amount added to the ones before.
fact: Cumulatively is an adverb, so it tells you how something is counted or happens.
fact: «Cumulatively, the team scored 200 goals» means every goal from every game is added into one total.
fact: A good check is to ask: does the total grow as each new part is added?
exercise: «Cumulatively, the rain filled the pond.» What does cumulatively suggest? | The rain stopped at once | Only one very loud storm | The pond was already empty | *All the showers added together, building up over time
exercise: Maya saved 2 pounds on Monday, 3 pounds on Tuesday and 5 pounds on Wednesday. How much had she saved cumulatively by Wednesday? | 2 pounds | *10 pounds | 5 pounds
exercise: «Small savings, cumulatively, make a big difference.» Why is this true? | *Each small amount is added to the total, so the total grows big | Small savings never change | Small amounts disappear over time | Big differences always start small and stay small
test: Which word means all added together, one after another? | Quickly | *Cumulatively | Barely | Curtly
test: Sam ran 1 km, then 2 km, then 3 km. Cumulatively, how far did he run? | *6 km | 3 km | 2 km | 1 km
exam: Tom reads 5 pages each night. Cumulatively, after a week, how many pages has he read? | 7 pages | *35 pages | 10 pages | 5 pages
